/* Meridia's geography, measured off the real Cursed Scroll 6 map spread.

   Pages 2-3 of the PDF are single full-page bitmaps (no text layer at all), 1796x2933 and
   1815x2933. Stitched side by side they join seamlessly into the printed spread, 3611x2933.
   That is the ONLY correct geometry for this map: an earlier version of this file rendered the
   PDF *pages* instead of the embedded images, which stretched everything horizontally by 14.5%
   (aspect 1.410 against the true 1.231) and is why the old hitboxes drifted away from the art.

   `public/meridia-map.png` is that stitched spread with the 50 numbered badges and the 9 district
   name plates painted out, cropped to the city's ink bounding box, and converted to white line art
   on transparency. Every coordinate below is in the cropped image's own pixel space, so the art and
   the overlay share one system with no offset or scale factor between them.

   MAP_PINS were found by detecting the printed badges themselves - solid black discs of radius 32,
   located as peaks of the distance transform of the filled ink - then read off a montage of crops
   to attach each number, then verified by drawing all 50 back onto the art quadrant by quadrant.

   BUILDINGS are ink blobs, not enclosed white regions. The cartographer draws each structure as a
   closed outline with its detail - roof ridge, dormers, windows, room divisions - drawn inside and
   touching it, so one building is one connected clump of ink and its footprint is that clump with
   its holes filled. A previous pass extracted the enclosed white regions instead, which is why a
   hipped roof came out as two buildings and a terrace came out as one building per room, and why
   most of the city read as missing. Non-buildings are rejected by measurement rather than by hand:
   a flooded shoreline or curtain wall is far larger than any drawn building, a pond or park is one
   enormous hole rather than many small rooms, and foliage has a wiggly outline (high
   perimeter^2/area). Polygons are simplified to 2.5px, well under a drawn wall's thickness.
   Each building is tagged with the district of its nearest numbered location, which reproduces the
   printed dashed boundaries closely and also drives the district highlight, so the wash is correct
   by construction rather than hand-traced.

   Footprints are traced with the numbered badges STILL PRESENT AS INK. An earlier pass painted a
   white disc of radius 41 over every badge before tracing, which cut the outline of whatever
   building the badge sat on - so up to 50 buildings, at exactly the 50 places the book cares
   about, were damaged or dropped, and no footprint could ever contain a pin (every pin measured
   ~40px outside the nearest edge, which is just the paint-out radius). A badge is a solid disc
   drawn ON its building, so leaving it as ink merges it into that building's blob: the footprint
   comes out whole and the pin lands inside it. Only the 9 district name plates are painted out.
   The shipped PNG is unchanged and still badge-free - painting pixels white never moved anything.

   `loc` is the book location this footprint IS, attached by containment only: 43 of the 50 pins
   land inside a footprint, which is proof. The other 7 are left unattached rather than guessed at,
   because they are not single buildings - #4 Gedgarrin is a seven-tower college precinct, #13 The
   Duke's Bridge is a bridge, #28 Tree Grove is a grove, #21 Montmar Castle is a walled precinct,
   #18 The Grand Gate is a gate, #11 River Rats is a wharf, #50 Rump Roast's badge sits in clear
   paper. Attaching by proximity instead put Montmar Castle and The Grand Gate onto innocent
   little neighbours, so don't reintroduce it. Those 7 still open their book entry from the pin.
*/

